.\" Man page for the tk CLI.
.\" View with: man -l man/tk.1
.TH TK 1 "May 2026" "" "tk Manual"
.SH NAME
tk \- agent-first work tracking command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tk
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.br
.B tk
\fIcommand\fR
[\fIarguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tk
is an agent-first tool for managing backend-agnostic work items through a
local-first Repository Store and explicit backend Promotion.
.PP
This manual documents the command surface available in the current binary.
Future v1 commands are intentionally omitted until they are implemented.
.PP
New
.B Tickets
are local by default. A configured Remote does not make new Tickets
backend-backed; Promotion is the explicit boundary where local state becomes
backend intent.
.PP
A
.B Scope
narrows
.B tk next
and
.B tk list
to one Epic. Pass it as the optional positional
.I epic-id
argument or set
.BR TK_SCOPE ;
the argument wins. A Scope covers the named Epic and its child Tickets, and
naming a Ticket instead is an error. Absent a Scope, both commands consider the
whole Repository Store.
.B tk
neither stores a Scope nor infers one from git state, so a command's Scope is
visible in the command line or its environment.
.PP
.B tk search
and
.B tk grep
ignore Scope. Both cover the whole Repository Store, so a lookup never misses
because a Scope was active.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print top-level help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the current version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Repository setup
.TP
.B tk init
Initialize the Repository Store for the current git repository.
.PP
The Repository Store is a SQLite database at
\fI<git-common-dir>/tk/tk.db\fR. It is shared by all git worktrees for the
same repository and is untracked local state.
.PP
.B tk init
is idempotent. If the store already exists and is current, it reports the
existing path instead of recreating it.
.SS Creating Tickets and Epics
.TP
\fBtk add\fR [\fB\-\-bug\fR | \fB\-\-epic\fR] [\fB\-\-parent\fR \fIepic-id\fR] [\fB\-\-priority\fR \fIP0..P4\fR | \fB\-\-triage\fR] (\fB\-m\fR \fIparagraph\fR... | \fB\-F\fR \fIfile\fR)
.TQ
\fBtk add\fR [\fB\-\-bug\fR | \fB\-\-epic\fR] [\fB\-\-parent\fR \fIepic-id\fR] [\fB\-\-priority\fR \fIP0..P4\fR | \fB\-\-triage\fR] \fB\-F\fR \fB\-\fR
Create a local task Ticket by default.
.PP
.B \-\-bug
creates a bug Ticket.
.B \-\-epic
creates an Epic.
.B \-\-bug
and
.B \-\-epic
are mutually exclusive.
.B \-\-parent
places a new Ticket under an Epic; it is invalid with
.BR \-\-epic .
.B \-\-priority
sets local-only Priority for Tickets. The default is
.BR P2 .
.B \-\-triage
captures the Ticket in triage with no Priority, pending a decision; it is
invalid with
.B \-\-priority
or
.BR \-\-epic .
A triage Ticket is excluded from
.B tk next
until accepted with
.BR "tk accept \fIid\fR \-\-priority \fIPn\fR" .
.PP
.B \-m
and
.B \-\-message
are repeatable message paragraphs.
.B \-F
and
.B \-\-file
read the message from a file. Use
.B \-
to read the message from standard input. Editor mode for bare
.B tk add
is deferred from v1.
.PP
The first paragraph becomes the title. Later paragraphs become the body. An
empty message aborts creation.
.PP
On successful Ticket creation,
.B tk add
prints:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Created Ticket: <display-id> - <title>
Kind: task|bug
Priority: P0|P1|P2|P3|P4
Status: open
Parent: <epic-display-id>
.fi
.RE
.PP
A triage Ticket has no Priority, so it replaces the
.B Priority:
line with
.BR "Selection: triage" .
The
.B Parent:
line is printed only when
.B \-\-parent
is supplied, and it uses the parent Epic's current Display ID even when the
argument was an Alias.
.PP
On successful Epic creation,
.B tk add
prints:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Created Epic: <display-id> - <title>
Status: open
.fi
.RE
.SS Reading the List Tree
.TP
\fBtk list\fR [\fB\-\-ready\fR | \fB\-\-blocked\fR | \fB\-\-active\fR | \fB\-\-triage\fR | \fB\-\-parked\fR] [\fB\-\-local\fR | \fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-epic\fR] [\fIepic-id\fR]
Render the Repository Store List Tree.
.PP
The default view shows open and active Tickets and Epics. Epics render as
top-level rows, child Tickets render under their Epic with tree glyphs, and
unparented Tickets render as top-level rows.
.PP
.B \-\-ready
shows open Tickets with no unresolved Dependencies or External Blockers.
.B \-\-blocked
shows open or active Tickets with unresolved Dependencies or External Blockers.
.B \-\-active
shows active Tickets and Epics, retaining inactive Epics as containers for
active child Tickets.
.B \-\-triage
shows open Tickets in triage \(em captured work not yet accepted.
.B \-\-parked
shows open Tickets parked out of automatic selection \(em accepted work
intentionally held. The view flags are mutually exclusive.
.PP
.B \-\-local
shows only local items.
.B \-\-remote
shows only Backend-backed items \(em those that entered the Repository
Store through Adopt or Promotion.
.PP
.B \-\-epic
restricts the listing to Epics and composes with any view or Origin filter.
.PP
.B \-\-epic
and the positional
.I epic-id
are different tools despite the shared word.
.B \-\-epic
filters by item class \(em it shows Epics instead of Tickets.
.I epic-id
is a Scope \(em it narrows the listing to one Epic and its child Tickets.
.PP
When a Scope is active,
.B tk list
prints a hint above the tree so a narrowed view never reads as the whole
store:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Scope: project-1 (Epic + child Tickets)
.fi
.RE
.PP
When the earliest unresolved Mutation in the Mutation Log is failed or
applying,
.B tk list
prints a banner above the tree (or above the empty-view line) naming its
Mutation Sequence, state, and target Display ID:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Sync: Mutation 7 failed on gh-12 (tk sync log 7)
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pending head prints no banner, since that is the ordinary state between
syncs. The named item may sit outside the active Scope or outside the
current view \(em the banner reports where the Mutation Log is stuck, not
which rows are in view.
.PP
In every view a Ticket whose Selection State is not the default
.B accepted
carries a dim badge \(em
.B [triage]
for captured work not yet accepted,
.B [parked]
for accepted work held out of automatic selection. Accepted Tickets carry no
badge.
.PP
A row whose Item carries a Mutation the Backend has not yet received shows a
marker immediately before the title \(em ~ for a pending Mutation, ⚑ for a
failed one, or ⚑ ~ when both apply. The marker renders even on a done row,
since a queued or failed Mutation means the Backend does not yet agree with
the Item's current state; only the summary legend below is conditional \(em
it names a
.B Mutations:
line only when at least one row carries a marker.
.PP
On success with one local Ticket carrying an edit queued behind a Promotion
that has not applied yet,
.B tk list
prints:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
○ project-1 ● P2 ~ Investigate flaky login retry
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Total: 1 item (1 open)

Status: ○ open  ◐ active  ✓ done
Blocked: ⊘ blocked
Mutations: ~ pending
.fi
.RE
.SS Searching by title
.TP
\fBtk search\fR \fIQUERY\fR
Find Tickets and Epics whose title contains
.I QUERY
as a case-insensitive literal substring.
.PP
Search covers the whole Repository Store and every Item Status, including
done; it ignores Scope and is never narrowed by TK_SCOPE. It matches title
text only: exact Display ID or Alias lookup is
.BR tk\ show .
Matches render flat, without List Tree nesting, reusing the
.B tk list
row rendering and summary chrome, ordered by creation.
.PP
.I QUERY
is a single required positional matched literally, so
.B %
and
.B _
carry no special meaning. Use
.B \-\-
to search a query that begins with a dash (\fBtk search \-\- \-v\fR).
.PP
When nothing matches,
.B tk search
prints a no-match line and exits 0:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
No items match "auth".
.fi
.RE
.PP
On success it prints the matching rows followed by the List Tree summary. A
done Ticket queued for a status update to the Backend still carries its
marker \(em closing it locally does not mean the Backend agrees yet:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
○ project-1 [epic] Auth rework
◐ project-2 ● P2 Add auth middleware
✓ project-3 ● P2 ~ Auth token refresh
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Total: 3 items (1 open, 1 active, 1 done)

Status: ○ open  ◐ active  ✓ done
Blocked: ⊘ blocked
Mutations: ~ pending
.fi
.RE
.SS Searching content
.TP
\fBtk grep\fR [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-F\fR] [\fB\-C\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-A\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-B\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-q\fR | \fB\-c\fR] \fIPATTERN\fR
Find Tickets and Epics whose title or body matches the regular expression
.I PATTERN
and render each match as a
.BR "tk show" -style
block with the body collapsed to the matching lines plus surrounding context.
.PP
Grep covers the whole Repository Store and every Item Status, including done;
like
.B tk search
it ignores Scope and is never narrowed by TK_SCOPE. It searches title and body
text \(em content search, as opposed to the title-only lookup of
.B tk search
or the exact Display ID / Alias lookup of
.BR tk\ show .
.PP
.I PATTERN
is a Rust regular expression (RE2-family, like \fBgrep \-E\fR; no
backreferences or lookaround) and matching is case-sensitive by default. Matched
text is highlighted on a terminal. Matches print in creation order, never ranked,
with three lines of context each side by default,
.B \-\-
between non-contiguous hunks within one item, and a blank line between items.
An empty pattern, or an invalid regular expression, is a usage error
(a whitespace pattern is a valid needle, matched like \fBgrep\fR).
.PP
.B \-i
matches case-insensitively (full Unicode case folding), and
.B \-F
matches
.I PATTERN
as a literal string instead of a regular expression.
.PP
.B \-C
sets the context window to
.I N
lines each side (default 3);
.B \-A
and
.B \-B
override the trailing and leading side independently, and
.B "\-C 0"
shows only the matching lines.
.PP
.B \-q
suppresses all output and exits on the first match;
.B \-c
prints the count of matching items instead of the blocks. Both keep the
exit-status predicate described below.
.PP
.B tk grep
overloads its exit status as a \fBgrep \-q\fR\-style predicate: it exits 0 when
at least one item matches and 1 when none do, leaving stderr empty so a script
can tell \(lqno match\(rq from a real failure. A usage error exits 2.
.PP
On success it prints one
.B tk show
\-style block per matching item:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
○ project-2 · Add middleware
  P2 · Task · Created: 2026-06-04
  the handler validates the auth token
.fi
.RE
.SS Selecting Ready Work
.TP
\fBtk next\fR [\fIepic-id\fR]
Select the next ready Ticket.
.PP
.B tk next
selects open Tickets with no unresolved Dependencies or External Blockers. It
orders ready Tickets by Effective Priority, then own Priority, then Repository
Store created_seq, and never selects Epics. Effective Priority is the lowest
of a candidate's own Priority and the Priorities of every unfinished item it
transitively blocks through Dependencies and Epic-membership, so a ready
Ticket that gates a higher-Priority Blocked Item outranks lower-priority
direct work. Effective Priority does not cross the Scope boundary, so the same
Ticket can rank differently scoped and unscoped.
Backend Tickets use local import order for the created_seq tie break, not
backend-native creation time. Selection is deterministic and does not
randomize among candidates. Ticket Kind and Assignees do not affect ordering.
.PP
.B tk next
does not explain skipped candidates. When the selected Ticket's Effective
Priority is lower than its own Priority,
.B tk next
writes one rationale line to standard error in the form
.RS 4
.nf
<display-id>: Effective Priority <P> (via <contributor-id>)
.fi
.RE
so the non-obvious pick is explainable without a follow-up
.BR "tk show" .
Standard output stays a single Display ID line, so
.B id=$(tk next)
captures only the selection; redirect standard error with
.B 2>/dev/null
to suppress the rationale.
.PP
Parent Epic status, Dependencies, and External Blockers do not hide otherwise
ready child Tickets.
.PP
Mutation Log, Mutation Failure, and Sync Cursor state do not filter
.B tk next
selection, and
.B tk next
does not emit sync-health warnings.
.PP
.B tk next
is read-only and does not change Item Status. Use
.B tk start
to begin work on the selected Ticket.
.PP
An optional positional
.I epic-id
scopes selection to that Epic and its child Tickets, falling back to
.B TK_SCOPE
when the argument is absent. Absent both,
.B tk next
considers every ready Ticket in the Repository Store. A Ticket supplied where
an Epic is expected is a failure, not a single-item selection.
.PP
.B tk next
answers with one Ticket. To inspect the whole ready set instead, use
.BR "tk list --ready" .
.PP
On success,
.B tk next
prints one Display ID:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<display-id>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B tk next
has no JSON or structured-output mode in v1; the Display ID line is the
machine interface.
.PP
If no ready Ticket matches,
.B tk next
returns exit status 1 and reports on standard error that nothing is ready,
naming the Epic when a Scope was active. A Scope that names no known item, or
names a Ticket rather than an Epic, fails the same way without selecting
anything.
.SS Backend Promotion and recovery
.TP
\fBtk promote\fR \fIid\fR [\fB\-\-children\fR]
Create a Backend object for a Local Ticket or Epic through the configured
Remote. The outgoing local Display ID remains an Alias after the Backend
Display ID replaces it.
.PP
.B \-\-children
includes an Epic's directly contained Local Tickets in the same Promotion
Operation. Promotion preflight rejects the whole operation before any Backend
write when its Item classes, Ticket Kinds, Dependencies, or Epic membership
cannot be represented.
.TP
\fBtk promote reconcile\fR \fIid\fR \fIbackend-key\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
Recover a Promotion whose Backend creation outcome is indeterminate. tk
inspects the candidate Backend object and by default requires its title and
body to match the retained Promotion snapshot exactly.
.PP
.B \-\-force
accepts a content mismatch only after the operator confirms the candidate.
The confirmed identity is attached atomically and current local title and body
are queued to converge the Backend object. Normal Backend Pull remains
authoritative for lifecycle state after attachment. A Backend object another
Item already tracks is refused with or without
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
\fBtk promote retry\fR \fIid\fR
Explicitly return a Promotion with an indeterminate creation outcome to pending
and run normal ordered sync. Use this only when creating the Backend object
again is safe; automatic sync never replays an indeterminate creation. A failed
Promotion is refused, because ordinary sync already retries it.
.PP
Reconcile and retry cannot pass an earlier pending, failed, or applying
Mutation. Both hold the Remote workflow lock through Backend access, Store
changes, and nested sync, then print every Promotion Display ID replacement
that nested sync landed.
.TP
\fBtk promote cancel\fR \fIid\fR
Withdraw the whole Promotion Operation the named item's Promotion belongs to,
together with every queued Mutation that cannot be applied without a withdrawn
item's backend identity. Cancelled items become Local again, keeping their
current state, Display IDs, and Aliases; promoting them again is an ordinary
Promotion.
.PP
A pending or failed Promotion is cancelled, because neither created a Backend
object. An applying Promotion is abandoned instead: tk never observed what its
creation did, so the withdrawal reports that any object it created is now
untracked and cannot name it. Find such an object upstream and adopt or close
it. A Promotion the Backend already accepted is reported and left in place —
tk never deletes a Backend object.
.PP
Cancellation is refused while a withdrawal would leave a backend-backed Blocked
Item waiting on an item returning to Local; drop the Dependency with
.B tk unblock
first. Epic membership degrades to local instead. Cancellation reaches no
Backend, so it works with a broken or unconfigured Remote and is not held back
by an earlier unresolved Mutation. Withdrawn Mutations are recorded as
cancelled and listed by
.BR "tk sync log \-\-cancelled" ;
a Promotion withdrawn while its outcome was unobserved is recorded as abandoned
and listed by
.BR "tk sync log \-\-abandoned" .
.PP
Promoting an item again after an abandonment reports that this promotion may
create a second Backend object, because the first outcome was never observed.
.SS Agent briefing
.TP
.B tk prime
Print static agent workflow guidance to standard output when the current
directory has an initialized Repository Store.
.PP
.B tk prime
exits successfully with no output when there is no initialized Repository
Store, including outside a git repository, so a global agent hook can run it
in any directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Logical failure, such as missing Repository Store or invalid message input.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Unexpected internal error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TK_SCOPE
Supplies a Scope to
.B tk next
and
.BR "tk list" .
The value is the Display ID or Alias of an Epic. A positional
.I epic-id
argument overrides it; unset or empty means no Scope.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Any non-empty value suppresses styled output.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
Any non-empty value forces styled output even when the stream is not a
terminal.
.PP
Styling is resolved separately for standard output and standard error, so
piping one stream does not silence the other.
.B NO_COLOR
wins over
.BR CLICOLOR_FORCE ,
which wins over terminal detection; a stream that is not a terminal is
unstyled by default. A terminal that cannot render SGR escape sequences
receives plain text rather than literal escape bytes.
.SH EXAMPLES
Initialize a repository-local store:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk init
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a local Ticket from a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk add -F followup.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a local Ticket from standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk add -F - <<'EOF'
Investigate flaky login retry

Repro is intermittent on the staging cluster.
First seen 2026-04-30.
EOF
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create an Epic and a child Ticket:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk add --epic -m "Jira backend"
tk add --parent project-1 -m "Map Jira issue fields"
.fi
.RE
.PP
List visible work:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk list
tk list --ready
.fi
.RE
.PP
Narrow the same views to one Epic and its child Tickets:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk list project-1
tk next project-1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select the next ready Ticket:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk next
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work through one Epic without restating it on every command. Exporting the
Scope keeps it in the environment, so it survives across a long unattended
run:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
export TK_SCOPE=project-1
while id=$(tk next); do
    tk start "$id"
    # ... implement ...
    tk done "$id"
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print agent guidance:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk prime
.fi
.RE
